  "summary": "Imperial architecture of the Ottonian Renaissance characterized by massive proportions and decorative elements.",
  "description": "Features thick walls, small windows, and sculpted portals with symbolic imagery. Developed as an expression of imperial power and renewed connection to classical traditions after the Carolingian period.",
  "culturalContext": "Ottonian architecture (936-1024) — the Saxon emperors' church-building program — bridged Carolingian and Romanesque: Hildesheim's St. Michael (Bernward's church) with its double-choir, double-transept \"Saxon\" symmetry, Gernrode's alternating pier-and-column nave, and massive westworks (westwerk) as imperial entry stages. The Ottonian Renaissance's architecture expressed sacral kingship: two choirs meant two liturgical fronts, emperor and God facing each other through the nave.",
